{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve the given problem, we start by analyzing the equation \\(z^2 + z + 1 = 0\\). The roots of this equation are the non-real cube roots of unity, specifically \\(z = \\omega\\) and \\(z = \\omega^2\\), where \\(\\omega = e^{2\\pi i / 3} = -\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}i\\) and \\(\\omega^2 = e^{-2\\pi i / 3} = -\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}i\\). These roots satisfy \\(\\omega^3 = 1\\) and \\(\\omega^2 + \\omega + 1 = 0\\).\n\nFirst, we need to find the value of \\(z + \\frac{1}{z}\\). Since \\(z = \\omega\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\omega^2 + \\omega + 1 = 0 \\implies \\omega^2 + \\omega = -1 \\implies \\omega + \\frac{1}{\\omega} = -1.\n\\]\nSimilarly, for \\(z = \\omega^2\\):\n\\[\n\\omega^4 + \\omega^2 + 1 = 0 \\implies \\omega + \\omega^2 = -1 \\implies \\omega^2 + \\frac{1}{\\omega^2} = -1.\n\\]\nThus, for any \\(z = \\omega^k\\) where \\(k \\equiv 1, 2 \\pmod{3}\\), we have:\n\\[\nz + \\frac{1}{z} = -1.\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the value of \\(z^2 + \\frac{1}{z^2}\\). For \\(z = \\omega\\):\n\\[\n\\omega^2 + \\frac{1}{\\omega^2} = -1.\n\\]\nSimilarly, for \\(z = \\omega^2\\):\n\\[\n\\omega^4 + \\frac{1}{\\omega^4} = \\omega + \\omega^2 = -1.\n\\]\nThus, for any \\(z = \\omega^k\\) where \\(k \\equiv 0 \\pmod{3}\\), we have:\n\\[\nz^2 + \\frac{1}{z^2} = -1.\n\\]\n\n","prefix_end":1177,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":false,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,279,2661,3491,11,582,1191,553,41018,279,23606,17767,89,61,17,488,1147,488,220,16,284,220,15,59,568,576,19703,315,419,23606,525,279,2477,74795,23739,19703,315,30326,11,11689,17767,89,284,1124,32696,57758,323,17767,89,284,1124,32696,61,17,59,701,1380,1124,11520,32696,284,384,47822,17,59,2493,600,608,220,18,92,284,481,59,37018,90,16,15170,17,92,488,1124,37018,35702,26888,90,18,3417,90,17,92,72,57758,323,1124,11520,32696,61,17,284,384,87210,17,59,2493,600,608,220,18,92,2